{:edb/db-before-t 2428 :edb/db-after-t 2429 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535741 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:25:46.705Z" 13194139535741 true] [17592186157051 1100 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n\n- The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n\n- The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head" 13194139535741 true] [17592186157051 1100 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n1. The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n2. The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n3. The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head" 13194139535741 false] [17592186157051 1145 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the die is rolled before $4$ appears for the first time can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, but the number of times that $2$ appears cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Tossing a coin is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the coin is tossed before the first head appears can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535741 true] [17592186157051 1145 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the die is rolled before $4$ appears for the first time can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, but the number of times that $2$ appears cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Tossing a coin is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the coin is tossed before the first head appears can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535741 false] [17592186157052 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shots until the archer misses. Since the chance of missing each shot is $7\\%$, the number of shots until the archer misses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 7\\% = \\dfrac{7}{100} = 0.07\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.07)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.07)^{x - 1}(0.07) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{x - 1}(0.07)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 8) &= f(8) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{8 - 1}(0.07) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{7}(0.07) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.042\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 false] [17592186157052 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shots until the archer misses. Since the chance of missing each shot is $7\\%,$ the number of shots  until the archer misses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 7\\% = \\dfrac{7}{100} = 0.07.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.07).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.07)^{x-1}(0.07) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{x-1}(0.07)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=8) & =f(8) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{8-1}(0.07) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{7}(0.07) \\\\  & ≈0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186157053 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%$, the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.1)^{x - 1}(0.1) \\\\\n&= (0.9)^{x - 1}(0.1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) + f(3) \\\\\n&= (0.9)^{1 - 1}(0.1) + (0.9)^{2 - 1}(0.1) + (0.9)^{3 - 1}(0.1) \\\\\n&= (0.1) + (0.9)(0.1) + (0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\\n&= [1 + 0.9 + (0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.271\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 false] [17592186157053 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%,$ the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.1)^{x-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{x-1}(0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{1-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{2-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{3-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.1)+(0.9)(0.1)+(0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =[1+0.9+(0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.271\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186157054 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%$, the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.35)^{x - 1}(0.35) \\\\\n&= (0.65)^{x - 1}(0.35)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3)$, we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) + f(3) \\\\\n&= (0.65)^{1 - 1}(0.35) + (0.65)^{2 - 1}(0.35) + (0.65)^{3 - 1}(0.35) \\\\\n&= (0.35) + (0.65)(0.35) + (0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\\n&= [1 + 0.65 + (0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.725\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 3) &= 1 - P(X \\le 3) \\\\\n&\\approx 1 - 0.725 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.275\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 false] [17592186157054 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%,$ the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq  3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.725 \\\\  & ≈0.275\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186157248 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364263 13194139535741 true] [17592186157248 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364270 13194139535741 true] [17592186157248 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364277 13194139535741 true] [17592186157248 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364284 13194139535741 true] [17592186157248 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364343 13194139535741 true] [17592186157249 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364291 13194139535741 true] [17592186157249 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364312 13194139535741 true] [17592186157249 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364315 13194139535741 true] [17592186157249 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364319 13194139535741 true] [17592186157250 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364298 13194139535741 true] [17592186157250 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364305 13194139535741 true] [17592186157250 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364322 13194139535741 true] [17592186157250 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364325 13194139535741 true] [17592186157251 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364329 13194139535741 true] [17592186157251 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364332 13194139535741 true] [17592186157251 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364335 13194139535741 true] [17592186157251 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364339 13194139535741 true] [17592186157805 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535741 true] [17592186157805 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAt a water bottle factory, every batch of $50$ water bottles has $4$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle they check? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 false] [17592186157805 1100 "At a water bottle factory, every batch of $50$ water bottles has $4$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle they check? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186157805 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bottles checked until a defect is found. On average, the chance of finding an upside down label on any water bottle is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{50}=0.08.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of bottles that the inspector checks before finding an upside down label can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.08.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.08).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.08)^{x-1}(0.08) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{x-1}(0.08)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{5-1}(0.08) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{4}(0.08) \\\\  & ≈0.057\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186157806 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186157806 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $15%$ chance of having a typo. What is the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences since the previous typo? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 false] [17592186157806 1100 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $15%$ chance of having a typo. What is the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences since the previous typo? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186157806 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $15%,$ the number of sentences since the previous typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=15%=\\frac{15}{100}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}](0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.386\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186157808 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535741 true] [17592186157808 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $5%$ chance of containing a typo. What is the probability of finding the first typo in the $4$th sentence or later? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 false] [17592186157808 1100 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $5%$ chance of containing a typo. What is the probability of finding the first typo in the $4$th sentence or later? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186157808 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $5%,$ the number of sentences checked until the first typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=5%=\\frac{5}{100}=0.05.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.05).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.05)^{x-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{x-1}(0.05)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require $P(X\\ge 4).$ \n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{1-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{2-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{3-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.05)+(0.95)(0.05)+(0.95)^{2}(0.05) \\\\  & =[1+0.95+(0.95)^{2}](0.05) \\\\  & ≈0.143,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, there are no typos within the first three sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.143 \\\\  & ≈0.857\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364263 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364264 13194139535741 true] [17592186364263 1089 "q-130456" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364263 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186364263 1100 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of candies selected with replacement from a bag before selecting a strawberry-flavored candy.\n- The number of messages received by a person before receiving a message from their boss\n- The number of strawberry flavored candies in a bag" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364263 1113 #uuid "5bb084c8-23d4-587b-b32c-ac9b0bef2c31" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364263 1145 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Selecting a candy and checking if it's a strawberry candy is a binomial experiment, and the number of candies selected before the first strawberry candy is selected can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II follows a geometric distribution. Receiving a message and checking if it's from the boss is a binomial experiment, and the number of messages received before receiving the first message from the boss can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The number of candies (of some flavor) in a bag is not a binomial experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364265 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364266 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364267 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364268 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364269 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1041 "selection" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364268 13194139535741 true] [17592186364264 1110 #uuid "b23579f5-5f18-5e6e-8d8f-bad1a303333b" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364265 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364265 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364265 1111 #uuid "cbc2ff1e-85fa-5309-b1fe-6d4a4cb0c8c5" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364266 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364266 1111 #uuid "671400ca-64c9-52ce-ab82-68fc2dd19191" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364267 1034 "I only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364267 1111 #uuid "64ad1679-5054-5383-9f13-01fcbed29e45" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364268 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364268 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364268 1111 #uuid "1aed5b0f-7a0b-56f3-98b5-bd43762657ca" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364269 1034 "II only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364269 1111 #uuid "e993cea8-c3b1-55e3-b2a5-dca0e4a24e9b" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364270 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364271 13194139535741 true] [17592186364270 1089 "q-130481" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364270 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186364270 1100 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of ice creams sold at an ice cream shop before a chocolate flavor ice cream is sold\n- The price of a cone at an ice cream shop\n- The number of ice cream flavors at an ice cream shop" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364270 1113 #uuid "15af2569-5af6-588a-90b7-d692ca6f6aea" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364270 1145 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Checking if a sold ice cream is chocolate is a binomial experiment, and the number of ice cream sold before the first chocolate ice cream flavor is sold can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The price of a cone of ice cream is not a binomial experiment.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The number of ice cream flavors at an ice cream shop is not a binomial experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364272 13194139535741 true] [17592186364271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364273 13194139535741 true] [17592186364271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364274 13194139535741 true] [17592186364271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364275 13194139535741 true] [17592186364271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364276 13194139535741 true] [17592186364271 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535741 true] [17592186364271 1041 "selection" 13194139535741 true] [1