 variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Reviewing a file is a binomial experiment, and the number of files reviewed before the first infected file is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Finding an infected file is a binomial experiment, but the number of infected files in a computer cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Randomly picking out a crayon is a binomial experiment, and the number of crayons picked out before a blue crayon is picked can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364279 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364280 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364281 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364282 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364283 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1041 "selection" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364280 13194139535741 true] [17592186364278 1110 #uuid "4de825f7-0447-53f5-b85a-6e0112744178" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364279 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364279 1111 #uuid "9c85153c-eb9f-550b-b019-defd84334f28" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364280 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364280 1111 #uuid "5b503cab-5b6a-55a8-a286-2202aa79873e" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364281 1034 "I only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364281 1111 #uuid "ebee5405-1c8c-5d37-a7e3-207dadaf3e48" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364282 1034 "III only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364282 1111 #uuid "aa51c0ad-dc04-575e-af4e-4abd53549441" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364283 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364283 1111 #uuid "c45850c2-8228-56b4-ba02-1015d5d01010" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364285 13194139535741 true] [17592186364284 1089 "q-130490" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364284 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186364284 1100 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of people examined at a clinic during a day\n- The average age of the people infected with a virus in a certain city\n- The number of people examined at a clinic until a person infected with a certain virus is found" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364284 1113 #uuid "47b6467e-8e21-5c39-9860-e9cd104f0a35" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364284 1145 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Being examined is a binomial experiment, but the number of people examined cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The average age of the people infected with a virus in a certain city is not a binomial experiment.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Being examined is a binomial experiment, and the number of people examined until a person infected with a certain virus is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364286 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364287 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364288 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364289 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364290 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1041 "selection" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364289 13194139535741 true] [17592186364285 1110 #uuid "3413d292-c623-5b07-a4a2-6c010f8ec6d6" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364286 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364286 1111 #uuid "f0915fab-6449-54dd-9c87-dcd39ad30a1d" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364287 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364287 1111 #uuid "e4224f64-e376-5151-8f7a-93b3b18c248c" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364288 1034 "I only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364288 1111 #uuid "8a001fc3-08b0-54d7-9147-5b40c722c472" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364289 1034 "III only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364289 1111 #uuid "21124fd1-44c5-539c-bb5b-6f90bcd70277" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364290 1034 "II only" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535741 true] [17592186364290 1111 #uuid "043d1d9a-8ba7-51c5-b5db-627f2d9aa4f9" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364291 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364292 13194139535741 true] [17592186364291 1089 "q-130654" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364291 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186364291 1100 "At an ice cream shop, on average, $25%$ of customers buy chocolate ice cream. What is the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first person buying a chocolate ice cream? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364291 1113 #uuid "38accb43-fafe-5993-aaff-60454863470a" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364291 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that enter the ice cream shop until the first one buys a chocolate ice cream. Since the chance of a customer buying a chocolate ice cream is $25%,$ the number of customers before the first buys a chocolate ice cream can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first buying a chocolate ice cream is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{4-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{3}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364293 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364294 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364295 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364296 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364297 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1041 "selection" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364294 13194139535741 true] [17592186364292 1110 #uuid "78f5b325-c8e0-51e2-a32c-d3c1e4d6d14c" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364293 1034 "0.012" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364293 1111 #uuid "738e9fe7-758d-538d-99a5-43e3154c8684" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364294 1034 "0.105" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364294 1111 #uuid "5d2bd36c-2038-5df0-a72f-cb89ebc1db30" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364295 1034 "0.079" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364295 1111 #uuid "46829c30-f4d2-553c-be56-35200bdbad75" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364296 1034 "0.422" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364296 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364296 1111 #uuid "e86ed7e0-ad2c-5c3d-bce5-e51439877bcf" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364297 1034 "0.316" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364297 1111 #uuid "122b3357-e311-5596-8293-ec0fe4af6744" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364298 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364299 13194139535741 true] [17592186364298 1089 "q-130785" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364298 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535741 true] [17592186364298 1100 "A card is being randomly picked from a standard $52$ cards deck and put back in until getting a red card. What is the probability of choosing a red card within the first $5$ tries? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364298 1113 #uuid "0dff2f00-8aa3-55d8-bf33-223d5ef1133d" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364298 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards picked until a red one is chosen. Since there are $26$ red cards in a $52$ cards deck, the chance of choosing a red card is $\\frac{26}{52}.$ Thus, the number of chosen cards before getting a red card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{26}{52}=\\frac{1}{2}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a red card within the first $5$ tries is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{1-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{2-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{5-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)+(0.5)(0.5)+(0.5)^{2}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4}(0.5) \\\\  & =[1+0.5+(0.5)^{2}+(0.5)^{3}+(0.5)^{4}](0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364300 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364301 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364302 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364303 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364304 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1041 "selection" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364303 13194139535741 true] [17592186364299 1110 #uuid "3cb94663-bd1f-59a4-854e-78c066f6a910" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364300 1034 "0.984" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364300 1111 #uuid "1b5684cb-4319-5f98-9ad1-f9c7b636cc22" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364301 1034 "0.938" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364301 1111 #uuid "40a605f7-25ff-5da4-90d3-03ebaaaf5913" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364302 1034 "0.492" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364302 1111 #uuid "5c7c9b4c-39ef-5793-b881-a24f2c954901" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364303 1034 "0.969" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364303 1111 #uuid "7b3191cc-7442-521c-ae12-3b06ca961483" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364304 1034 "0.469" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364304 1111 #uuid "f668fbe4-65c7-552a-bcd1-45113e40664d" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364305 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364306 13194139535741 true] [17592186364305 1089 "q-130789" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364305 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186364305 1100 "A coin is being randomly picked from a jar and put back in until getting a one-dollar coin. The jar holds a $70%$ of one-dollar coins. What is the probability that a one-dollar coin is chosen before the $5$th trial?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364305 1113 #uuid "6a9f89b2-2f50-5518-8fed-2bb94691ea23" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364305 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of choices before choosing a one-dollar coin. Since the chance of choosing a one-dollar coin is $70%,$ the number of choices required to choose a one-dollar coin can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a one-dollar coin before the $5$th trial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{1-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.7)+(0.3)(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =[1+0.3+(0.3)^{2}+(0.3)^{3}](0.7) \\\\  & ≈0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364307 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364308 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364309 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364310 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364311 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1041 "selection" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364309 13194139535741 true] [17592186364306 1110 #uuid "938c80e6-e65f-5baf-a378-58c31a57d081" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364307 1034 "0.292" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364307 1111 #uuid "c12ddaf1-af07-5a49-ad1a-589bb50fe49e" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364308 1034 "0.990" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364308 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364308 1111 #uuid "22c673e1-d9a2-5157-b1c5-6621b638843f" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364309 1034 "0.992" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364309 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364309 1111 #uuid "b53f04c4-288a-584e-8329-0bcbcce4f8dc" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364310 1034 "0.994" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364310 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364310 1111 #uuid "6f173a74-e851-5ddf-b9fa-705ffb7c4acf" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364311 1034 "0.417" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364311 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364311 1111 #uuid "51b0b2f7-1889-5893-9c1c-45df695af231" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364312 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364313 13194139535741 true] [17592186364312 1089 "q-276119" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364312 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535741 true] [17592186364312 1100 "A fair six-sided die is repeatedly rolled until the number six appears. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will be rolled exactly $2$ times is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364312 1113 #uuid "86b032f4-16fc-5c91-979b-b3292c008241" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364312 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of rolls until a $6$ came on. The probability of getting a $6$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of rolls until a $6$ came up can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{6}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a $6$ on the $2$nd roll is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2-1}(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ≈0.139,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364314 13194139535741 true] [17592186364313 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364313 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364313 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364314 13194139535741 true] [17592186364313 1110 #uuid "21555e84-3242-5158-84cc-6af27270f754" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364314 1034 "0.139" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364314 1111 #uuid "94e8b3c2-05ed-5826-975e-4e53f8c5bda3" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364315 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364316 13194139535741 true] [17592186364315 1089 "q-332311" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364315 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186364315 1100 "A customer service representative resolves a caller’s issue with a $45%$ chance during each minute of a call. The call continues until the issue is resolved. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ minutes to resolve the issue is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364315 1113 #uuid "06c35c2b-90c4-512d-8ffb-dd48cb36067f" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364315 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of minutes until the representative resolves the issue. Since the chance of resolving the issue in any given minute is $45%,$ the number of minutes until the issue is resolved can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=45%=\\frac{45}{100}=0.45.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.45).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.45)^{x-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{x-1}(0.45)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the representative will resolve the issue for the first time in the $5$th minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{5-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{4}(0.45) \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364317 13194139535741 true] [17592186364316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364318 13194139535741 true] [17592186364316 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364316 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364316 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364317 13194139535741 true] [17592186364316 1110 #uuid "c71b40ef-e9a8-5540-ac05-59a402934269" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364317 1034 "0.041" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364317 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364317 1111 #uuid "0f53ba58-0fad-58ff-80b5-37b911e678d0" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364318 1034 "0.045" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364318 1111 #uuid "9c3e16d9-e049-5366-b36f-65319a4d9811" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364319 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364320 13194139535741 true] [17592186364319 1089 "q-332319" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364319 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535741 true] [17592186364319 1100 "A bag contains $10$ balls numbered $1$ through $10.$ One ball is randomly selected, its number is recorded, and then it is placed back in the bag before the next selection. The process is repeated until the ball numbered $10$ is selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ selections is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364319 1113 #uuid "c938a148-e37e-5951-8e76-aecc10b0a6a7" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364319 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of selections until the ball numbered $10$ came on. The probability of getting a ball numbered $10$ on any single selection is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections until the ball numbered $10$ came up can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{10}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a ball numbered $10$ on the $5$th selection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{5-1}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{4}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364321 13194139535741 true] [17592186364320 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364320 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364320 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364321 13194139535741 true] [17592186364320 1110 #uuid "f05036cc-1990-5361-bfee-21e3e089d536" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364321 1034 "0.066" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364321 1111 #uuid "91d14a7c-30de-5757-9b09-bcc2dfe00a45" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364322 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364323 13194139535741 true] [17592186364322 1089 "q-332334" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364322 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186364322 1100 "A smartphone receives a software update that has a $60%$ chance of installing successfully on any given attempt. The phone keeps trying until the update installs successfully. Rounded to $2$ decimal places, the probability that the update installs successfully within the first $2$ attempts is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364322 1113 #uuid "c71a9582-a35f-5a15-92d1-d7c3959c34c7" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364322 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of attempts until the update installs successfully. Since the chance that the update installs successfully on a single attempt is $60%,$ the number of attempts required to install the update can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=60%=\\frac{60}{100}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.6).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.6)^{x-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{x-1}(0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the update installs successfully in one of the first $2$ attempts is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{1-1}(0.6)+(0.4)^{2-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =[1+(0.4)](0.6) \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364324 13194139535741 true] [17592186364323 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364323 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364323 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364324 13194139535741 true] [17592186364323 1110 #uuid "99a065a0-fbaa-55c8-a1b9-c5ef8fcb7755" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364324 1034 "0.84" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364324 1111 #uuid "aca1fa90-d1e3-5664-bdd1-d0da7089e74d" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364325 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364326 13194139535741 true] [17592186364325 1089 "q-332342" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364325 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535741 true] [17592186364325 1100 "A company monitors incoming emails for spam. On average, $18$ out of every $150$ emails are spam messages. The emails are checked one by one until a spam email is found. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that a spam email is found before checking the $4$th email is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364325 1113 #uuid "790cfa57-4b15-5033-b8b1-c8b7382d10ca" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364325 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of emails checked until a spam email is found. On average, the chance that any single email is spam is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{18}{150}=0.12.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of emails that are checked before finding a spam email can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.12.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.12).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.12)^{x-1}(0.12) \\\\  & =(0.88)^{x-1}(0.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding a spam email before checking $4$ emails is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.88)^{1-1}(0.12)+(0.88)^{2-1}(0.12)+(0.88)^{3-1}(0.12) \\\\  & =(0.12)+(0.88)(0.12)+(0.88)^{2}(0.12) \\\\  & =[1+0.88+(0.88)^{2}](0.12) \\\\  & ≈0.319,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364327 13194139535741 true] [17592186364326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364328 13194139535741 true] [17592186364326 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364326 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364326 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364327 13194139535741 true] [17592186364326 1110 #uuid "3bcb67b8-7996-5f0f-b704-f39753e824e1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364327 1034 "0.319" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364327 1111 #uuid "15baed1a-9acc-5f10-9ca4-60671036da8a" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364328 1034 "0.400" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364328 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364328 1111 #uuid "daf983f6-f253-55dd-ada7-fabf54dce528" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364329 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364330 13194139535741 true] [17592186364329 1089 "q-332354" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364329 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535741 true] [17592186364329 1100 "A board game uses a special deck of $10$ cards: $4$ cards show a star and $6$ cards show a circle. A player randomly draws a card, records the symbol, and then puts the card back into the deck before drawing again. The player keeps drawing until they get a star card. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364329 1113 #uuid "9d6a06a7-8856-5c78-a6ce-7249e45a932d" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364329 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards drawn until a star card is obtained. The chance of selecting a star card from the deck is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections before obtaining a star card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{5}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1-1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2-1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3-1}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =[1+(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}](\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =[1+\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{9}{25}](\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =0.784.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.784 \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364330 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364331 13194139535741 true] [17592186364330 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364330 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364330 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364331 13194139535741 true] [17592186364330 1110 #uuid "3570967b-d850-5a69-9d4b-d5bbcc27ec69" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364331 1034 "0.216" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364331 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364331 1111 #uuid "5abb4464-d39d-50dc-85e3-e9dfea531718" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364332 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364333 13194139535741 true] [17592186364332 1089 "q-332355" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364332 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535741 true] [17592186364332 1100 "In a medical clinic, a certain rapid test detects a particular infection. On average, $9$ out of every $300$ patients who take the test receive a positive result. Each patient’s test result is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364332 1113 #uuid "28c160b2-1422-5a1c-9b8b-0a7bcfaab4c6" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364332 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients tested until the first positive result is obtained. Since the chance that any single patient tests positive is $3%,$ the number of patients tested until the first positive result can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{300}=0.03.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.03).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.03)^{x-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{x-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>6),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>6)=1-P(X\\le 6)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{1-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{2-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3-1}(0.03) \\\\  & \\,+(0.97)^{4-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{5-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{6-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =[1+(0.97)+(0.97)^{2}+(0.97)^{3}+(0.97)^{4}+(0.97)^{5}](0.03) \\\\  & ≈0.167,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.167 \\\\  & ≈0.833,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364334 13194139535741 true] [17592186364333 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364333 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364333 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364334 13194139535741 true] [17592186364333 1110 #uuid "60e85f82-c79d-5023-b86a-469069558919" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364334 1034 "0.833" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364334 1111 #uuid "98d938b6-af68-5946-80a5-00573839d85f" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364335 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364336 13194139535741 true] [17592186364335 1089 "q-332356" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364335 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535741 true] [17592186364335 1100 "A coffee shop tracks how often customers order a latte. On average, $30%$ of customers order a latte. Each customer’s order is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first customer who orders a latte is at least the $4$th customer is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364335 1113 #uuid "03ab75ca-b53c-5714-b488-f3426966ee5d" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364335 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers served until a latte is ordered. On average, the chance that any single customer orders a latte is \n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of customers before the first latte order can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.3.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-0.3)}^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & ={(0.7)}^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{3-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3)+(0.7)^{2}(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7+(0.7)^{2}](0.3) \\\\  & =0.657.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first customer who orders a latte is at least the $4$th customer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.657 \\\\  & =0.343.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364337 13194139535741 true] [17592186364336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364338 13194139535741 true] [17592186364336 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364336 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364336 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364338 13194139535741 true] [17592186364336 1110 #uuid "9cc057dd-2d6d-5d90-8463-efde2e69fe11" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364337 1034 "0.657" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364337 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364337 1111 #uuid "680caf6f-1369-5805-92bc-f0d996ced42b" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364338 1034 "0.343" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364338 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364338 1111 #uuid "79a47645-8871-59d1-b4f7-0b638c456ddc" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364340 13194139535741 true] [17592186364339 1089 "q-332361" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535741 true] [17592186364339 1100 "A parcel delivery company records whether each delivered package requires a signature upon arrival. On average, $9$ out of $50$ packages require a signature. Each package's status is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first package requiring a signature is the fifth package delivered or later is{{field-1}}." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364339 1113 #uuid "0cbb4bce-278b-52c7-885c-e42015c98fe6" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364339 1145 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of packages delivered until a package that requires a signature is delivered. On average, the chance that any single package requires a signature is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{50}=0.18.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of packages until the first package that requires a signature is delivered can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.18.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.18).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-0.18)}^{x-1}(0.18) \\\\  & ={(0.82)}^{x-1}(0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 5),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=1-P(X<5)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{1-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{2-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{4-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =(0.18)+(0.82)(0.18)+(0.82)^{2}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3}(0.18) \\\\  & =[1+0.82+(0.82)^{2}+(0.82)^{3}](0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.548,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that at least $4$ packages are delivered before the first package that requires a signature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.548 \\\\  & ≈0.452,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535741 true] [17592186364340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364341 13194139535741 true] [17592186364340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364342 13194139535741 true] [17592186364340 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535741 true] [17592186364340 1041 "field-1" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364340 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364341 13194139535741 true] [17592186364340 1110 #uuid "44dd1966-e341-521a-b2f9-b984a8b92579" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364341 1034 "0.452" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364341 1111 #uuid "fe926947-6944-5427-9d9e-85887ab5fea4" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364342 1034 "0.548" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364342 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535741 true] [17592186364342 1111 #uuid "a7883ff5-ae60-5666-896a-830ccf255aa2" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364343 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364344 13194139535741 true] [17592186364343 1089 "q-85838" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364343 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535741 true] [17592186364343 1100 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of times we draw a red card from a $52$-card deck\n- The number of people interviewed about whether they have a dog until one person says they have a dog\n- The number of cards that are selected from a deck with replacement before an ace is drawn" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364343 1113 #uuid "3532479f-3afe-51f3-a4f7-455c2ef6c17d" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364343 1145 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Drawing a card from a deck is a binomial experiment, but the number of times we draw a red card cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II follows a geometric distribution. Interviewing a person about whether or not they have a dog is a binomial experiment, and the number of people interviewed before the first person with a dog is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Drawing a card from a deck with replacement is a binomial experiment, and the number of cards selected from a deck with replacement before the first ace is drawn can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535741 true] [17592186364344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364345 13194139535741 true] [17592186364344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364346 13194139535741 true] [17592186364344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364347 13194139535741 true] [17592186364344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364348 13194139535741 true] [17592186364344 1000 #edb/ref 